  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA shortwave extending from the base of the main mid-level trough \nwill continue to move across South Texas today, interacting with \nincreasing moisture to support isolated to scattered showers. Rain \nchances have been increased slightly this afternoon to account for \nongoing activity and the potential for additional development as the \ndisturbance progresses eastward. Despite sufficient positive \nvorticity advection, weak mid-level lapse rates have so far become \nmarginally conducive for thunderstorm activity and have had few \nlightning strikes on radar early this afternoon. The major concern \nlies with the potential for heavy rainfall and flash flooding. \n\nAttention then turns to Sunday as a cold front approaches South \nTexas from the north and east, with the latest guidance suggesting a \nfrontal passage Sunday afternoon into evening. Increasing \nsoutheasterly flow ahead of the boundary will transport deeper Gulf \nmoisture into the region, with PWAT values forecast to peak around \n1.50-1.75 inches Sunday afternoon, according to the GFS, \napproximately 2 std deviations above normal. Combined with \nincreasing instability, surface convergence, and large-scale \nascent associated with the approaching trough, conditions will \nbecome increasingly favorable for widespread showers and \nthunderstorms, particularly across the eastern Coastal Plains and \nVictoria Crossroads. \n\nOf particular concern is the potential for heavy rainfall and \nflash flooding through Sunday evening. Recent HRRR and RRFS \nguidance continues to favor the development of heavier convective \ncells across the Victoria Crossroads and generally east of Corpus \nChristi Bay. Rainfall totals of 2-4 inches are forecast across the\nVictoria Crossroads region, with localized amounts reaching 5+ \ninches where repeated rounds of convection or training \nthunderstorms occur. Elsewhere across South Texas, rainfall \namounts generally range from 1-2 inches or less, tapering off \ntowards the west. \n\nIn addition to heavy rainfall, a few strong to severe thunderstorms \nwill be possible Sunday, particularly across the Victoria \nCrossroads. Increasing instability and effective shear exceeding 40 \nknots could support organized clusters or marginal supercellular \nstructures capable of damaging winds, hail, and perhaps a brief \ntornado. SPC maintains a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe \nthunderstorms, although uncertainty remains regarding storm \nevolution and exact timing. The cold front will push through by late \nSunday, followed by strong northeasterly winds and cooler \ntemperatures. Rain chances diminish by late Sunday/early Monday \nmorning, with breezy conditions and highs mainly in the 60s for \nMonday. Dry weather continues through midweek before onshore flow \nreturns, bringing warmer temperatures and low rain chances (20-30%) \nlate in the week. \n\nMinor coastal flooding remains possible tonight, although confidence \nhas decreased, with water levels forecast to reach 1.8-2.0 feet MSL. \nIncreasing swell periods and strengthening winds ahead of and behind \nthe cold front will also contribute to an increased rip current risk \nand dangerous swimming and beach conditions along the Gulf waters.",
